Pata was declared National Cultural Heritage of Peru in 2003. A 2015 restoration initiative involving the Cusco Department of Culture and local communities began clearing vegetation to reveal the site\\''s full structural extent.'' -> ''Wat\\''a (Quechua for \"island\") is an archaeological zone in the Cusco Region, Anta Province, Huarocondo District, situated atop the Pachar ravine north of Huarocondo [1] [2]. The complex comprises five sections: Wat\\''aq\\''asa, Willkapata, Qhawarina, Qullqa, and Saksaywaman Pata, featuring enclosures, platforms, and ceremonial sites [1] [2]. Saksaywaman Pata was declared National Cultural Heritage of Peru in 2003 [1]. A 2015 initiative involving the Cusco Department of Culture and local communities began clearing vegetation to reveal the site\\''s full structural extent [2] [3].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0abf05cb-0fda-4567-9d9d-0829911a8c66'::uuid, 'antiquity', '10', '5', 'card-stats-2026-10-04b-rollback:0abf05cb-0fda-4567-9d9d-0829911a8c66:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-10-04b: antiquity ''5'' restored on Paradise Cave', '8fdced857102f7b6b61841795a4d92d2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 10 from period_start -126000"}, {"source": "production export a991f0242bf4f80c (2026-10-04 18:08:44.180088+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 133 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04''s own export (2026-10-04 13:55:46.282309+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33043", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0012:chunk-0001: description ''Paradise Cave (Jaskinia Raj) is a karstic limestone cave near Kielce, Poland, and one of the most important Palaeolithic sites in the Swietokrzyskie region. The cave served as a Neanderthal encampment approximately 60,000-70,000 years ago, with excavations in the 1960s yielding over 600 flint artifacts and animal remains including horse, mammoth, and rhinoceros bones. Recent research has uncovered hundreds of well-preserved prehistoric bones from cave lions, bears, and mammoths in just five square feet of excavation. The cave also contains spectacular calcite formations with approximately 47,000 stalactites, and has been open to visitors since 1972.'' -> ''Paradise Cave is a horizontal karstic limestone cave located inside the Malik hill, to the south of Kielce, Świętokrzyskie Voivodship, Poland [1]. The cave was discovered in 1963 by Józef Kopeć and Feliks Wawrzeńczak, students of a local technical school [1]. In the sub-recent cave deposits, there are traces of occupancy by Neanderthal dating back to the late Pleistocene, 50 to 60 thousand years ago [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_chan