    ('0b8a8edd-8a60-4758-985c-bf354de4a985'::uuid, 'mystery', '10', '9', 'card-stats-2026-10-04b-rollback:0b8a8edd-8a60-4758-985c-bf354de4a985:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-10-04b: mystery ''9'' restored on King Lud''s Entrenchments and The Drift', 'e8e1eecf6de0819c8e9223a35ad5d6db', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 10 from (''Earthwork'', None) is shared by 1 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export a991f0242bf4f80c (2026-10-04 18:08:44.180088+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 133 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04''s own export (2026-10-04 13:55:46.282309+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33047", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0012:chunk-0001: description ''King Lud\\''s Entrenchments is a Scheduled Ancient Monument and 23.9-hectare Site of Special Scientific Interest straddling the Lincolnshire-Leicestershire border, east of Croxton Kerrial. The earthworks consist of three parallel banks separated by two ditches extending for 1,387 m, with ditches up to 1.5 m deep and 8 m wide. Excavation revealed V-shaped and U-shaped ditch profiles. Once thought to be Anglo-Saxon, the earthworks are now believed to be prehistoric, possibly part of a large boundary system extending from Northamptonshire to the Humber. The traditional name \"Foulding Dykes\" suggests possible use for livestock management.'' -> \"King Lud''s Entrenchments and The Drift is a 23.9 hectares biological Site of Special Scientific Interest which straddles the border between Lincolnshire and Leicestershire, and is east of Croxton Kerrial [1]. King Lud''s Entrenchments is a scheduled monument [1]. King Lud''s Entrenchments is also known as King Lud''s Intrenchments or King Lud''s Banks [1]. The site has not been excavated and its date is unknown [1]. It has been thought of as a Saxon boundary ditch, but aerial photographic work has suggested that it may be part of an extensive prehistoric boundary system extending from Northamptonshire to the Humber and termed `the Jurassic spine'' [1]. This site has limestone grassland with tor-grass, cock''s foot, crested dog''s-tail and red fescue [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:113527", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:113528", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
