ces to a height of 4 metres, surrounded by a U-shaped ditch over 2 metres deep [1]. The outwork lies 100 metres to the east and is 17.8 metres wide and 0.4 metres high [1]. Remains found on the site suggest it was subject to an attack by the Romans, although the circumstances are unrecorded, and may simply reflect use of an abandoned site for training purposes [1]. Hawkesdown Camp was designated as a scheduled monument in 1924 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95365", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95366", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302104", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302105", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_start None -> ''-800''"}]'::jsonb),
