uction that spread through much of Great Britain, Ireland, and Brittany between 3,300 and 900 BCE, during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age [1]. The Nine Stones circle is positioned at the national grid reference 36100904, on the western edge of the village of Winterbourne Abbas in Dorset [1]. The roots of a beech tree have engulfed two of the megaliths in the circle [1]. Seven of the nine surviving stones are under 90 centimetres tall, but two of the northwestern stones are considerably larger [1]. The circle is located at the bottom of a narrow valley [1]. In the nineteenth century the site was visited by the antiquarian Charles Warne, who wrote about it in his 1872 book Ancient Dorset [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35766", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-3300'' -> ''-4500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84222", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26a_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84223", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26a_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149521", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3b/The_Winterbourne_Abbas_Nine_Stones_-_geograph.org.uk_-_25044.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/50f9ccc8/Nine_Stones,_Dorset.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302122", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302123", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_start None -> ''-2500''"}]'::jsonb),
