    ('5b27b64f-8c83-4685-8644-8a2076d83322'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-10-04b-rollback:5b27b64f-8c83-4685-8644-8a2076d83322:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-10-04b: mystery ''6'' restored on Bela Palanka', 'f3b2b72986e40a0c0ee9fd9834cade4b', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''City/town/settlement'', None) is shared by 8 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export a991f0242bf4f80c (2026-10-04 18:08:44.180088+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 133 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04''s own export (2026-10-04 13:55:46.282309+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:80898", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2012:chunk-0001: description ''Bela Palanka (\"White Town\") in southeastern Serbia occupies the site of ancient Remesiana, originally a Dacian settlement called Aiadava. After the Roman conquest of Moesia, it became the municipium of Remesiana on the Via Militaris between Naissus and Serdica. The site is notable as the seat of Saint Nicetas of Remesiana (c. 335-414 AD), an influential early Christian bishop canonized by both Catholic and Orthodox churches. Archaeologists have uncovered fortress remains, several basilicas, graves, jewellery, coins, and a well-preserved 3rd-century Roman milestone 2.4 m high. In 2012, the largest single-nave early Christian basilica ever found in Serbia was discovered nearby. The site was declared an Archaeological Site of Great Importance by Serbia in 1987.'' -> \"The name Bela Palanka means ''white town'' [1]. The town was originally settled by the Dacians and was known under the ancient name of Aiadava or Aeadaba [1]. After the Romans conquered Moesia in 75 BC, the new castrum and municipium was known initially as Ulpianorum and then Remesiana and stood along the Via Militaris between Naissus and Serdica [1]. The patron saint of Romania, Nicetas of Remesiana, was a 4th-century bishop at Remesiana [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:99951", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:99952", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:150357", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-012: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/5b27b64f/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/5b27b64f/BelaPalankaIskopine.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
