t part of the English Channel to the important religious complexes of Avebury and Stonehenge, in Wiltshire, where it is known as the Harroway [1]. The way then existed as \"broad and ill-defined corridors of movement up to half a mile wide\" and not as a single, well-defined track [1]. The route was still followed as an artery for through traffic in Roman times [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105216", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105217", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:158393", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-023: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Pilgrims%27_Way_to_the_South_of_the_White_Horse_Stone_%28I%29.jpg'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/a98dcb51/Pilgrims'' Way to the South of the White Horse Stone (II).webp\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241633", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241634", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: period_start None -> ''-600''"}]'::jsonb),
