s [1]. Subfossil bird bones have also been well preserved, providing much new information about the avifauna of the island around the time it was first settled by humans, demonstrating that even small islands could hold a rich variety of bird species [1]. Several excavations were conducted by Yosihiko H. Sinoto of the Bernice P. Bishop Museum [1]. Globally extinct species of land birds found at the site include two rails, the Huahine rail and swamphen; two doves, the great ground dove and Huahine cuckoo-dove; two parrots, the conquered lorikeet and Sinoto''s lorikeet, and the Huahine starling [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124255", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> ''1000 - 1500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124256", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''500'' -> ''1050''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124257", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124258", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: source_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fa%27ahia'' -> ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fa%CA%BBahia''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162183", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-028: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/cb637b1a/hero.webp'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
