]. French explorer Charles Huber saw the steles in situ in 1878 [1]. The first publication was made by Theodor Nöldeke on July 10, 1884, with information provided by Julius Euting [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124316", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: lat ''48.861301646608645'' -> ''27.62972''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124317", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: lon ''2.335844025900915'' -> ''38.54389''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124318", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124319", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:124320", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s009: site_type ''Rock relief/carving'' -> ''Inscription''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162872", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-029: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Teima_stone_Louvre_AO1505.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ce733a7d/Tayma stones (173).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cf7d3acb-1b74-484d-8084-6c7297b23ec0'::uuid, 'antiquity', '10', '5', 'card-stats-2026-10-04b-rollback:cf7d3acb-1b74-484d-8084-6c7297b23ec0:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-10-04b: antiquity ''5'' restored on Caves of Arcy-sur-Cure', 'e683249ae1b4bc92abdd414fcd44b05f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 10 from period_start -3000000"}, {"source": "production export a991f0242bf4f80c (2026-10-04 18:08:44.180088+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 133 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04''s own export (2026-10-04 13:55:46.282309+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45279", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0108:chunk-0001: description ''A system of 14 limestone caves in Burgundy, France, formed by the Cure river some 60 million years ago, constituting one of the richest archaeological assemblages in France with 200,000 years of human occupation. The Grotte du Renne yielded Neanderthal stone tools and ornaments from the Chatelperronian period (45,000-38,000 BCE), demonstrating significant cultural creativity, while a Neanderthal jawbone discovered in 1858 provided the first direct evidence of human occupation. The Grande Grotte preserves approximately 170 animal paintings in ochre and charcoal dating to over 28,000 years ago -- the second oldest after Chauvet -- depicting mammoths and stags in a distinctive continuous-contour style. The paintings were hidden beneath calcite and torch soot until revealed through infrared photography in the 1990s.'' -> ''The caves of Arcy-sur-Cure are a series of caves located on the commune of Arcy-sur-Cure, Burgundy, France [1]. Some of them contained archaeological artefacts, from the Mousterian to Gallo-Roman times [1]. Some hold remarkable parietal art, the second oldest presently known after those of the Chauvet Cave [1]. The caves are 1.3 km south of Arcy-sur-Cure, on the left bank of the river Cure [1]. Prehistoric parietal paintings were discovered by Pierre Guilloré in April 1990 in the Great cave [1]. Made on walls in cave rooms located 300 to 500 m away from the entrance, the paintings are 28,000 years old for the oldest, according to radiocarbon dating measures on charcoal remains discovered in these cave rooms in the corresponding strata [1]. Dug by the Cure river in a limestone massif from the Mesozoic, the caves were used as shelter by humans since at least 200,000 years ago [1]. The oldest traces of human presence were stone tools found at the bottom of the cave of the Hyena, from Neanderthals during the Mousterian (Middle Paleolithic, the middle part of the European Old Stone Age) [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108844", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108845", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_start ''-28000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162898", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-029: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/cf7d3acb/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/cf7d3acb/Arcy - Bison 1 - IMG 0390.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302197", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302198", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_start None -> ''-3000000''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cf7d3acb-1b74-484d-8084-6c7297b23ec0'::uuid, 'empire_count', '0', '2', 'card-stats-2026-10-04b-rollback:cf7d3acb-1b74-484d-8084-6c7297b23ec0:empire_count', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-10-04b: empire_count ''2'' restored on Caves of Arcy-sur-Cure', 'e683249ae1b4bc92abdd414fcd44b05f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "empire_count = 0 from tag_site(47.591869268488715, 3.7663674393206703, period_start -3000000)"}, {"source": "production export a991f0242bf4f80c (2026-10-04 18:08:44.180088+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 133 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59724 cells (basis: wave 2026-10-04''s own export (2026-10-04 13:55:46.282309+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45279", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0108:chunk-0001: description ''A system of 14 limestone caves in Burgundy, France, formed by the Cure river some 60 million years ago, constituting one of the richest archaeological assemblages in France with 200,000 years of human occupation. The Grotte du Renne yielded Neanderthal stone tools and ornaments from the Chatelperronian period (45,000-38,000 BCE), demonstrating significant cultural creativity, while a Neanderthal jawbone discovered in 1858 provided the first direct evidence of human occupation. The Grande Grotte preserves approximately 170 animal paintings in ochre and charcoal dating to over 28,000 years ago -- the second oldest after Chauvet -- depicting mammoths and stags in a distinctive continuous-contour style. The paintings were hidden beneath calcite and torch soot until revealed through infrared photography in the 1990s.'' -> ''The caves of Arcy-sur-Cure are a series of caves located on the commune of Arcy-sur-Cure, Burgundy, France [1]. Some of them contained archaeological artefacts, from the Mousterian to Gallo-Roman times [1]. Some hold remarkable parietal art, the second oldest presently known after those of the Chauvet Cave [1]. The caves are 1.3 km south of Arcy-sur-Cure, on the left bank of the river Cure [1]. Prehistoric parietal paintings were discovered by Pierre Guilloré in April 1990 in the Great cave [1]. Made on walls in cave rooms located 300 to 500 m away from the entrance, the paintings are 28,000 years old for the oldest, according to radiocarbon dating measures on charcoal remains discovered in these cave rooms in the corresponding strata [1]. Dug by the Cure river in a limestone massif from the Mesozoic, the caves were used as shelter by humans since at least 200,000 years ago [1]. The oldest traces of human presence were stone tools found at the bottom of the cave of the Hyena, from Neanderthals during the Mousterian (Middle Paleolithic, the middle part of the European Old Stone Age) [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108844", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108845", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s019: period_start ''-28000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162898", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-029: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/cf7d3acb/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/cf7d3acb/Arcy - Bison 1 - IMG 0390.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302197", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302198", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_start None -> ''-3000000''"}]'::jsonb),
